00:00:28.572 --> 00:00:31.090
A lot happened in the year 2022.

00:00:31.090 --> 00:00:34.879
Economies shifted, reigns ended, wars began.

00:00:34.879 --> 00:00:40.715
So it is okay if you missed out on this little
bit of we think very important news:

00:00:40.715 --> 00:00:44.305
 Demodex have buttholes.

00:00:44.305 --> 00:00:47.029
Now perhaps you have never thought 
to wonder about this.

00:00:47.029 --> 00:00:52.309
Perhaps you have never even heard of a Demodex 
before and you’re just slowly putting together

00:00:52.309 --> 00:00:55.760
that it must be related to the creature that 
we are watching right now.

00:00:55.760 --> 00:01:01.629
Yeah, the one that looks like a tardigrade
but weirder, which says a lot given that tardigrades

00:01:01.629 --> 00:01:03.542
already look pretty weird to us.

00:01:03.542 --> 00:01:08.619
So you might wonder why we would care if a
Demodex has a butthole or not.

00:01:08.619 --> 00:01:12.715
Well, we care because they live on our face.

00:01:17.811 --> 00:01:24.871
Demodex are a genus of tiny mites that build their 
lives around the hair follicles of various animals.

00:01:24.871 --> 00:01:30.566
And there are two species that are found on
humans: Demodex folliculorum and Demodex brevis.

00:01:30.566 --> 00:01:35.890
They each have their own preferred nooks on
our faces, with Demodex folliculorum preferring

00:01:35.890 --> 00:01:41.210
hair follicles, and Demodex brevis preferring
our sebaceous glands.

00:01:41.210 --> 00:01:46.490
The mites we are looking at had the distinction
of coming straight from the face of our master

00:01:46.490 --> 00:01:51.969
of microscopes, James, who saw a little black
dot on his forehead and was curious

00:01:51.969 --> 00:01:53.850
what was going on inside.

00:01:53.850 --> 00:01:59.141
So he simply scraped the spot with the side
of a microscope slide and took a look.

00:01:59.141 --> 00:02:04.780
But these mites don’t always localize themselves
in neat little dots, so sometimes scientists

00:02:04.780 --> 00:02:07.710
have to turn to other measures to study them.

00:02:07.710 --> 00:02:13.020
In one NPR interview, a scientist described
applying glue to a microscope slide and then

00:02:13.020 --> 00:02:17.849
sticking it to a person’s forehead so
as they peeled the slide off, the mites would

00:02:17.849 --> 00:02:19.487
peel off too.

00:02:19.487 --> 00:02:20.651
And look, we get it.

00:02:20.651 --> 00:02:25.639
Learning that there are little animals crawling 
around your face, it might be a little unsettling.

00:02:25.639 --> 00:02:30.990
Even James, who spends just about every day
thinking about microbes, found himself initially

00:02:30.990 --> 00:02:34.117
repulsed by what he found on his skin.

00:02:34.117 --> 00:02:38.213
But then he realized that they look like tardigrades
with big butts.

00:02:38.213 --> 00:02:44.520
And if we can all collectively love tardigrades
the way that we seem to, perhaps we can find some

00:02:44.520 --> 00:02:47.837
space in our hearts for these mites.

00:02:47.837 --> 00:02:51.159
They’re even cute under the harsh glare
of our UV light!

00:02:52.790 --> 00:02:56.159
The mites are tiny, less than a millimeter
in length.

00:02:56.159 --> 00:02:59.310
And you can see two distinct halves to their
body.

00:02:59.310 --> 00:03:04.740
At one end—the tardigrade end, as we like
to call it in a purely unscientific way—are

00:03:04.740 --> 00:03:08.933
eight stubby legs waving behind the Demodex’s
mouth.

00:03:08.933 --> 00:03:13.399
At the other end are their gastrointestinal
organs and their genitals.

00:03:13.399 --> 00:03:18.590
Those spherical droplets in their body are
oil droplets, the digested remains of the

00:03:18.590 --> 00:03:23.211
sebum and moisturizer that Demodex like to
eat from our skin.

00:03:23.211 --> 00:03:27.930
Now, if at this moment, your mind is conjuring
up images of the demodex wandering your face

00:03:27.930 --> 00:03:30.909
in search of oil to eat, you can rest easy.

00:03:30.909 --> 00:03:32.438
Well, for now at least.

00:03:32.438 --> 00:03:37.545
They’re probably not wandering, they're 
just chilling out inside your pores.

00:03:37.545 --> 00:03:41.141
At night though, Demodex do like to party.

00:03:41.141 --> 00:03:46.690
They emerge from your pores in search of each
other, looking for other mites to mate with.

00:03:46.690 --> 00:03:51.510
And when they are done, they head back to
your pores to lay their eggs.

00:03:51.510 --> 00:03:56.779
An adult Demodex folliculorum lays around
20-24 eggs in your hair follicles.

00:03:56.779 --> 00:04:03.519
Within three to four days, the young Demodex will 
hatch, emerging with only six legs weirdly enough.

00:04:03.519 --> 00:04:08.110
As they develop into adulthood, the other
two legs will  develop as well.

00:04:08.110 --> 00:04:12.980
Their life span will end a few weeks later,
at which point the dead mites will decompose

00:04:12.980 --> 00:04:19.165
in your follicles or sebaceous glands, turning
the tiny little pockets of your skin into

00:04:19.165 --> 00:04:21.808
little mite graveyards.

00:04:21.808 --> 00:04:23.140
It's a lot, I know.

00:04:23.140 --> 00:04:27.889
We keep trying to say that Demodex are cute
actually, but then we talk about little mite

00:04:27.889 --> 00:04:33.860
mating parties and mite graveyards on your
skin, and none of that is cute.

00:04:33.860 --> 00:04:36.320
But here’s the thing: we pretty much all
have them.

00:04:36.320 --> 00:04:39.960
If you’re watching this video, you probably
have Demodex living in your face.

00:04:39.960 --> 00:04:42.822
And if you don't now, you will eventually.

00:04:42.822 --> 00:04:47.650
Now they usually spread through physical contact,
though a 2015 study found that we tend to primarily

00:04:47.650 --> 00:04:52.179
pass them on via close contact with our family
members rather than strangers.

00:04:52.179 --> 00:04:56.539
And when they have found your face, they’ll
probably stick around, hiding in your pores

00:04:56.539 --> 00:05:00.398
and other spaces that make them hard to scrub
out.

00:05:00.398 --> 00:05:02.037
Generally, that’s fine.

00:05:02.037 --> 00:05:04.828
Demodex don’t wreak much havoc on your skin.

00:05:04.828 --> 00:05:10.294
They can become too abundant, creating a distinctive
white sheen that dermatologists call

00:05:10.294 --> 00:05:12.134
Demodectic frost.

00:05:12.134 --> 00:05:16.440
That excess of mites is usually connected
to a decline in the immune system.

00:05:16.440 --> 00:05:19.119
But these cases are very rare.

00:05:19.119 --> 00:05:24.160
For a time, there was a theory that Demodex
could cause rosacea, because they were thought

00:05:24.160 --> 00:05:26.059
to not have a butthole.

00:05:26.059 --> 00:05:28.405
What do those things have in common
with each other?

00:05:28.405 --> 00:05:32.391
Well the idea was that without an anus, 
the Demodex couldn’t poop.

00:05:32.391 --> 00:05:37.880
Instead, the Demodex would spend their short lives
accumulating waste in large cells in their

00:05:37.880 --> 00:05:40.000
body, and storing it away.

00:05:40.000 --> 00:05:46.300
And when they died, the feces would burst
from their bodies, releasing bacteria that

00:05:46.300 --> 00:05:48.687
could then cause rosacea.

00:05:48.687 --> 00:05:53.470
There are enough correlations between Demodex
and the occurrence of rosacea to leave us

00:05:53.470 --> 00:05:57.400
considering that there could be a connection
between the two.

00:05:57.400 --> 00:06:00.161
But we are here to vindicate the mites a little.

00:06:00.161 --> 00:06:05.431
Because that mechanism is based on an idea
that would turn out to be very wrong, the

00:06:05.431 --> 00:06:09.120
idea that Demodex don’t have an anus.

00:06:09.120 --> 00:06:13.530
In 2022, scientists were studying the genes
within Demodex.

00:06:13.530 --> 00:06:19.180
And among their results are some microscopy
images and these words, which we hope will

00:06:19.180 --> 00:06:23.730
become a classic in the history of butthole
science:

00:06:23.730 --> 00:06:29.000
There have been several reports that Demodex
does not have an anus, and when Demodex dies,

00:06:29.000 --> 00:06:34.711
the accumulated waste spills into the pores
of the skin and leads to inflammation; this

00:06:34.711 --> 00:06:36.484
is not correct.

00:06:37.305 --> 00:06:44.090
We’ve talked before about just how mysterious
and confusing the history of anus evolution

00:06:44.090 --> 00:06:48.550
is, and so we were excited to hear this news.

00:06:48.550 --> 00:06:53.430
But the other results of the scientist’s
genetic study of the Demodex are also worth

00:06:53.430 --> 00:07:01.740
learning more about because they reveal just how 
strange our relationship with these creatures is.

00:07:01.740 --> 00:07:07.660
In particular, the scientists found that the
Demodex are surprisingly simple, relying on

00:07:07.660 --> 00:07:11.090
a small number of proteins to survive.

00:07:11.090 --> 00:07:15.740
This likely drives their nocturnal behavior,
because they lack the compounds that allow

00:07:15.740 --> 00:07:20.548
their relatives to survive at all in the daytime.

00:07:20.548 --> 00:07:25.380
And this might be the result of just how
lonely the Demodex mites are, in an ecosystem

00:07:25.380 --> 00:07:30.431
built out of our skin where they face few
threats and little competition.

00:07:30.431 --> 00:07:36.423
They’re doing so well that they may require
less than they once did to survive.

00:07:36.423 --> 00:07:41.030
And that has been pushing them—at least according
to these scientists—to a future where they

00:07:41.030 --> 00:07:47.230
might be symbionts with us, much like bacterial
endosymbionts that have become fully embedded

00:07:47.230 --> 00:07:49.060
in their hosts.

00:07:49.060 --> 00:07:52.300
That is, for now, just a hypothesis.

00:07:52.300 --> 00:07:57.610
If there comes a day when the hypothesis is
fulfilled, well what do we do then?

00:07:57.610 --> 00:08:02.110
Do we consider them weird and creepy,
and maybe even a little rude?

00:08:02.110 --> 00:08:09.900
Or do we accept them as part of us, as inevitable
as every other cell in our body?

00:08:09.900 --> 00:08:15.174
Or perhaps it is both, as nuanced as any
other experience we have with our bodies.

00:08:15.174 --> 00:08:19.004
Wonderful and terrible, all at once.

